---
import Article from '../../components/Article.astro';

const title = 'Spades Strategy: How to Win (Bidding & Tactics) | CardHearth';
const description =
  'Practical Spades strategy: count your bid, bid nil, cover your partner, manage bags, and set the opposing team.';
const updated = '2026-06-14';
const heading = 'Spades Strategy: How to Win';
const subtitle =
  'Win more at Spades by bidding accurately — count your sure tricks, cover your partner, dodge the bag penalty, and know when nil is the right gamble.';

const breadcrumb = [
  { name: 'CardHearth', href: '/' },
  { name: 'Guides', href: '/guides/' },
  { name: 'Spades Strategy', href: '/guides/spades-strategy/' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How do I count my bid in Spades?',
    a: 'Count near-certain tricks: high spades (Ace, King, often Queen), side-suit aces, and protected side-suit kings. Add roughly one trick for a void or singleton in a side suit once you have a few spades to trump with. Round down when in doubt — being set costs far more than a missed bag.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I bid nil?',
    a: 'Bid nil when your hand can realistically duck every trick: no high spades, few or no spades at all, and low cards in the side suits (no bare aces or kings that must win). A void you can throw losers into helps. With a partner who can cover you, a clean nil is worth 100.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I avoid the bag penalty?',
    a: 'Bid close to your real trick count and then take only what you bid. Don’t grab overtricks you don’t need — duck low when a trick is already safe for your team. Watch your running bag total: as it nears 10, actively avoid winning extra tricks.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Spades Strategy: How to Win (Bidding & Tactics)',
    description,
    datePublished: updated,
    dateModified: updated,
    author: { '@type': 'Person', '@id': 'https://cardhearth.com/#jared', name: 'Jared Waterworth', url: 'https://cardhearth.com/about/' },
    publisher: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'CardHearth',
      url: 'https://cardhearth.com/',
      logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://cardhearth.com/logo.png' },
    },
    mainEntityOfPage: 'https://cardhearth.com/guides/spades-strategy/',
    image: 'https://cardhearth.com/og/spades.png',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: breadcrumb.map((c, i) => ({
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: i + 1,
      name: c.name,
      item: `https://cardhearth.com${c.href}`,
    })),
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
    })),
  },
];
---

<Article
  title={title}
  description={description}
  heading={heading}
  subtitle={subtitle}
  updated={updated}
  schema={schema}
  breadcrumb={breadcrumb}
  cta={{ href: '/spades/', label: 'Play Spades' }}
>
  <p>
    Spades is won at the bid. If you already know
    <a href="/guides/spades-rules/">the rules</a>, the leap to winning comes from counting your hand
    honestly, playing as a partnership, and respecting the bag penalty. Here is how to do all three.
  </p>

  <div class="tldr">
    <span class="tldr-title">Quick tips</span>
    <ul>
      <li>Bid the tricks you can actually win — when unsure, bid the lower number.</li>
      <li>Getting set costs 10 per bid trick; a bag only costs you later. Underbid, don't overbid.</li>
      <li>Stop taking tricks once your team has made its bid — ten bags is -100.</li>
      <li>If your partner bid nil, cover them: win the tricks they can't safely duck.</li>
      <li>Save high spades to capture aces and control the end of the hand.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>Count your bid honestly</h2>
  <p>
    Your bid should reflect tricks you can <strong>actually win</strong>, not tricks you'd like to.
    Start by counting your near-certain winners:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>High spades:</strong> the Ace and King are almost always tricks; the Queen often is.</li>
    <li><strong>Side-suit aces:</strong> usually good for a trick on the first round of that suit.</li>
    <li><strong>Protected kings:</strong> a King with at least one small card behind it will often score.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Trumping power:</strong> a void or singleton in a side suit, plus a few low spades to
      ruff with, is worth roughly an extra trick.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    When unsure between two numbers, <strong>bid the lower one</strong>. A bag costs you a future
    penalty at worst; getting <em>set</em> costs you ten points per bid trick right now.
  </p>

  <h2>Play as a partnership</h2>
  <p>
    Your two bids combine into one contract, so think about the team's total, not your own tricks.
    If your partner bid high, you may only need to chip in one or two — don't fight them for tricks
    they're set up to win. If your partner bid <strong>nil</strong>, your job changes entirely: you
    must <strong>cover</strong> them. Win tricks they can't safely duck, lead suits that let them
    throw their dangerous cards, and take the high cards so a forced win never lands on them.
  </p>

  <h2>Respect the bags</h2>
  <p>
    Overtricks feel like free points, but ten bags wipe out 100. Once your team has made its bid,
    stop grabbing tricks you don't need — play <strong>under</strong> when a trick is already going to
    your side, and dump high cards you'd otherwise be stuck winning with later. Keep an eye on the
    running bag count; near 10, ducking becomes a priority over a single extra point.
  </p>

  <h2>Manage your spades</h2>
  <p>
    Spades are your trump and your currency. Don't burn high spades early on side-suit tricks you
    could win another way — save them to capture opponents' aces and to control the end of the hand.
    Leading spades to <strong>draw</strong> them out can be powerful when you hold length, stripping
    opponents of trumps so your side-suit winners run. But remember you can't lead spades until
    they're broken.
  </p>

  <h2>Set the opponents</h2>
  <p>
    Defense wins games too. If the other team bid aggressively, you don't always need your own tricks
    — you need <em>theirs</em>. Force them to use trumps early, hold up your high cards to deny a key
    trick, and count how many they still owe. Setting a team that bid 8 swings 80 points your way in
    relative terms. Watch for an opponent's nil, too, and try to feed them a trick they can't avoid.
  </p>

  <h2>Adjust to the score</h2>
  <p>
    Late in a game to 500, bid for the situation. If you're well ahead, bid conservatively and play
    safe — don't hand the trailing team a chance to set you. If you're behind, look for a bold nil or
    an aggressive bid that can close the gap in one hand. And always know how close each team is to
    the next bag penalty.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked example: counting your bid</h2>
  <p>
    You pick up <strong>&spades;A K 4, &hearts;A, &diams;K 7 3, &clubs;Q 9 6 2</strong>. Count your real
    winners: the &spades;A and &spades;K are two near-certain tricks, the singleton &hearts;A is a third
    (and leaves you void to trump hearts later), and the &diams;K behind two small diamonds will often be a
    fourth. That's a solid <strong>bid of 4</strong> — not 5. The &clubs;Q is not a reliable trick, so don't
    count it; if it scores, it's a bag, which is a far cheaper mistake than getting set.
  </p>
  <h2>Common mistakes that cost you</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Overbidding to look strong — being set costs 10 points per bid trick, far more than a stray bag.</li>
    <li>Grabbing overtricks you don't need; every 10 bags wipes out 100 points.</li>
    <li>Fighting your partner for tricks they already have covered.</li>
    <li>Leaving a partner's nil exposed — failing to win the trick they can't duck.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {
    faq.map((f) => (
      <details class="faq">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">{f.q}</summary>
        <p class="mt-2 opacity-90">{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Keep going</h2>
  <p>
    Take it to the table and play <a href="/spades/">Spades</a> with a computer partner. New here? Start
    with the <a href="/guides/spades-rules/">Spades rules</a>, see how the two games differ in
    <a href="/guides/hearts-vs-spades/">Hearts vs Spades</a>, find more in our roundup of the
    <a href="/guides/best-free-card-games/">best free card games</a>, or browse the
    <a href="/guides/">guides hub</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
